# Brownfield deletes for the canvas rewrite (wave 3) — COMPLETED, now empty.
#
# This pillar retired two whole files:
#
#   canvas/drag_handler.ts — the drag preview it computed is now
#     scene/preview's ScenePreview, drawn by overlay_layer. Its
#     commit-on-pointer-up path was finding §2.1 (a plain click writes a
#     position edit and teleports the node to 0,0); wave 4's move_gesture
#     plus the move_edit builder replace it properly.
#
#   canvas/viewport.ts — replaced by ui_state's Viewport + viewport_math
#     (wave 2), which take a ContainerRect and so fix finding §2.16. Its
#     local Vec2 was the second definition of that type in the repo
#     (docs/relaxing-koine-constraints.md §3.3); core/geometry's is the
#     survivor.
#
# The 11 per-symbol DELETED entries that drove this are removed rather than
# left in place: once the files are gone the symbols are absent from gcode,
# and `archegraph plan` rejects a DELETED node whose symbol it cannot find
# ("DELETED node references symbol not in gcode"). Keeping them would mean
# this pillar could never be re-planned. Git history has the originals.
#
# Worth knowing for the next pillar that retires a whole file: the 11
# parallel per-symbol delete nodes each edited the same two files, and
# convergence fanned them in badly — drag_handler.ts landed with literal
# conflict markers committed into it and viewport.ts was left half-deleted.
# Both were resolved by hand with `git rm`, which is what the spec meant
# anyway. Prefer retiring a whole file in one node, or expect to clean up
# after convergence.
#
# canvas/selection.ts deliberately SURVIVES this pillar — inspector.tsx
# still consumes useSelection and the numeric ElementKind enum, and that
# migration belongs to inspector v2 in wave 5. See app.spec.textproto for
# the one-way bridge that keeps it consistent meanwhile.
